{"id":"9411b808-aef4-4a6a-bad2-513aa8d0a783","object":"chat.completion","created":1784962355,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children_by_parent = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_by_parent.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Initial retained set\\n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    \\n    # Symbol count for required symbols (though we never remove required symbols)\\n    # Not needed for correctness, but keep for completeness\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Sort candidates: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes_by_id.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    \\n    removed = []\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check children: any child still retained?\\n        children = children_by_parent.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(child_id in retained for child_id in children):\\n            continue\\n        # Verify required symbols remain (by construction true, but check)\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        ok = True\\n        for req in required_symbols:\\n            cnt = symbol_count[req]\\n            if cnt - (1 if sym == req else 0) < 1:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` based on the problem description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – essentially a reduction algorithm on a parse tree with nodes having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The required_symbols list must be preserved. The algorithm should consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes, never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children (i.e., children that are not removed). Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented after removal. Use node ID as tie breaker.\n\nWe need to return `removed_node_ids` (ordered list of removed nodes, in order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted list of IDs that remain). We must not mutate the case, use only Python standard library.\n\nLet's parse the payload: `nodes` list of dicts with id (string), parent (string or null), symbol (string), depth (int), removable (bool), utility (float). `required_symbols` list of strings (symbols that must be present in the retained nodes). The root is id \"root\" with parent null.\n\nThe algorithm: we need to simulate a reduction process: repeatedly consider candidates for removal, ordered by depth descending (deeper first), then by node ID ascending for tie? The description: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending? \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So yes depth descending. Then stable tie break: node ID (string). We need to process nodes in that order, and for each, if it's removable (removable == true) and it's not the root, and its symbol is not required, and all required symbols are still present after removal, and it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its children are also removed already? Or children that are not removed? Actually we need to ensure we don't remove a node that has retained children. So we can only remove a node if all its descendants are already removed or will be removed? But we process in depth order, so deeper nodes are considered first, so when we consider a node, its children (deeper) have already been considered and possibly removed. So we can check that none of its children (direct children) are in the set of retained nodes (or not yet removed). Since depth descending, children are processed before parent, so they have been evaluated. If any child is retained, the parent cannot be removed. Also need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the set of retained nodes. So we need to simulate removal: we can start with all nodes as retained. Then we iterate over candidate nodes (sorted by depth descending, then id ascending). For each candidate, if it's removable and not root and its symbol not in required_symbols, and if currently all its children (that exist in the original tree) are either already removed or not retained? Actually we need to check if any child is currently retained. We'll maintain a set of retained IDs. Initially all IDs are retained. When we decide to remove a node, we remove it from retained set and add to removed list. Before removal, we check: 1) node is removable, 2) node's symbol not in required_symbols, 3) node is not root, 4) none of its children (by parent field) are in retained set (i.e., all children are already removed, or never retained? but children have been processed earlier due to depth, so if a child was retained, it would not have been removed). Also after potential removal, we must ensure that all required symbols are still represented. So we need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node with that symbol. If after removal some required symbol becomes absent, we cannot remove. Also need to consider that root itself might have required symbol \"root\" which is required.\n\nAlso need to consider that nodes may have children that are not in the list? The tree is defined by parent references; we can build a mapping from parent id to list of children. Then for each node, we know its children. To avoid recomputing, we can precompute children mapping.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n1. Build dict nodes_by_id.\n2. Build children_by_parent (list of child IDs).\n3. Determine initial retained set = all IDs.\n4. Build set of required_symbols.\n5. Build set of required_node_ids? Not needed; we just need to ensure at least one node with each required symbol retained.\n6. Create list of candidate node IDs sorted by depth descending, then node ID ascending.\n7. For each node_id in sorted candidates:\n   - node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\n   - if not removable: skip\n   - if node_id == \"root\": skip\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip\n   - check children: children = children_by_parent.get(node_id, []). For each child_id in children, if child_id in retained set, then cannot remove because node has retained children.\n   - check required symbols: after removal, would all required symbols still exist? Compute current set of symbols from retained IDs (excluding current node). We can precompute a count of nodes per symbol, or check each required symbol. Since number of nodes is small, we can recompute each time or maintain counts. We'll maintain a dict symbol_count: for each symbol, number of retained nodes with that symbol. Initially count all. When removing a node, decrement its symbol. Check before removal if for that symbol, count > 1 (if symbol is required, else irrelevant). But careful: the removed node's symbol may be a required symbol, but we already skip symbols in required_symbols, so that case won't happen. However, if a required symbol appears multiple times, removing one node with that symbol might be okay as long as at least one remains. So we need to check each required symbol: the count after removal must be >= 1. Since we only remove nodes with non-required symbols, the required symbol count won't change, so it's always fine? Not necessarily: required symbols might be present on nodes that are not removable, or removable but with required symbol? Actually we skip nodes with symbol in required_symbols, so we never remove a node that directly provides a required symbol. But what if a node's symbol is not required, but its removal could affect the existence of required symbols? No, because required symbols are tied to nodes; removing a node with a non-required symbol does not affect nodes with required symbols. So the check is actually trivial: after removal, all required symbols are still present because we never remove nodes with required symbols. But there is a nuance: the root is \"root\" symbol and required, so it's never removed. So indeed, as long as we skip nodes with required symbol, the required symbols remain. However, consider a scenario where a required symbol appears on multiple nodes; we might remove some of them? But we skip all nodes with required symbol, so we don't remove any. So no issue. But what if a node's symbol is not required, but it is the only node that gives that symbol? That doesn't matter because it's not required. So we don't need to check required_symbols after removal? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must check, but it's automatically satisfied given our skip. However, to be safe, we still check.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Precompute symbol_count for all symbols (initial retained = all nodes).\n- For each candidate:\n   - if not removable or root or symbol in required_symbols: skip\n   - check children: if any child in retained: skip\n   - For removal: we know symbol is not required, so symbol_count for that symbol will decrease, but we only care about required symbols. So we can skip checking required symbols because they are unaffected. But to be thorough, we can check: all( (symbol_count[req] - (1 if node['symbol']==req else 0)) >= 1 for req in required_symbols ). Since node['symbol'] not in required_symbols, it's always >=1.\n\n8. Remove node: remove from retained set, decrement its symbol count, append to removed list.\n\n9. After processing all candidates, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained set). removed_node_ids = list in order of removal.\n\nReturn dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nEdge cases: what if multiple nodes have same id? Not expected. depth could be negative? Probably not. utility ignored? The algorithm seems based only on structure and removable flag and required symbols. The description mentions \"utility\" but no use. Possibly used in some other tasks but not here. The task says \"grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties\" – \"interestingness_preserved\" relates to required_symbols preservation. So our algorithm matches.\n\nNow implement solve(case) that takes the payload (dict with nodes list, required_symbols list). Return the result.\n\nBe careful about JSON serialization: use json.dumps? But the function signature is solve(case) and must return a JSON-serializable object (dict). So return dict.\n\nWrite Python code inside a string as value of \"implementation\". Escape as needed.\n\nNow produce the final answer as JSON object with key \"implementation\". Ensure no extra text."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2085,"completion_tokens":2500,"total_tokens":4585,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1981},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":37},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}